1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,484
Ravi committed an environment
file with a password.

2
00:00:07,524 --> 00:00:12,110
A commit is one saved snapshot of the
project, and a push sends commits to the

3
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:16,854
team's server. He pushed, noticed, deleted
the file in a later commit and pushed

4
00:00:16,894 --> 00:00:21,598
again. Then he did the one thing in this
story that deserves protection: he told

5
00:00:21,638 --> 00:00:22,072
someone.

6
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:25,879
His message contains three
claims. The branch is clean.

7
00:00:25,919 --> 00:00:31,107
It was only his branch. The repository is
private. Each claim is a hypothesis, and

8
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:35,953
none of the three has been tested. And
while you're reading his message, the

9
00:00:35,993 --> 00:00:39,268
password still works. Hold
on to those three claims.

10
00:00:39,308 --> 00:00:42,010
Each one meets its
evidence before the end.

11
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:47,698
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:47,738 --> 00:00:51,713
This is a debrief of Incident 3,
incidents/03-committed-secret.

13
00:00:51,753 --> 00:00:56,812
You need to have generated and attempted
it, against the clock, and written your

14
00:00:56,852 --> 00:01:00,891
message to the team. If you haven't,
please stop the video here.

15
00:01:03,231 --> 00:01:08,979
You know the procedure. Video 166 gave you
the six steps, and videos 167 and 168 the

16
00:01:09,019 --> 00:01:14,699
mechanics of a rewrite and of the stale
clone. This drill is the second scenario of

17
00:01:14,739 --> 00:01:20,281
the senior standard, and what it adds is
the shape of a real team's repository: a

18
00:01:20,321 --> 00:01:25,862
second branch that a colleague based on
the first, time pressure, and clones that

19
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:27,310
hold the old objects.

20
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:32,336
A branch is a name for a line of commits,
and a clone is one person's copy of the

21
00:01:32,376 --> 00:01:37,121
repository. The secret in the sandbox is
a dummy string made for the drill, and it

22
00:01:37,161 --> 00:01:38,522
says so in its own text.

23
00:01:38,862 --> 00:01:43,498
One thing this video can't show, and neither
can any check script: the first step.

24
00:01:43,538 --> 00:01:44,809
It isn't a Git command.

25
00:01:46,649 --> 00:01:48,228
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:48,568 --> 00:01:49,980
After this video you can:

27
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:53,935
Answer "I deleted the file, so the
branch is clean" with evidence.

28
00:01:54,275 --> 00:01:58,943
Run the response in order and say what must
never be claimed before it is verified.

29
00:01:59,283 --> 00:02:05,044
Scope the exposure: first commit, first
push, refs, tags, dependent branches.

30
00:02:05,384 --> 00:02:09,198
Carry out the rewrite where it is
warranted and verify every clone.

31
00:02:09,538 --> 00:02:13,443
Write the message to the team that says
exactly what to do with their clones.

32
00:02:15,283 --> 00:02:21,157
The order is fixed, and its first step
is not Git. Contain, assess, eradicate,

33
00:02:21,197 --> 00:02:23,432
recover, communicate, prevent.

34
00:02:23,772 --> 00:02:28,835
1. Contain: revoke or rotate the credential
first. Revoking cancels the credential,

35
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:33,692
and rotating replaces it with a new one.
The damage happens where the secret is

36
00:02:33,732 --> 00:02:38,610
accepted, and automated scanners copy
fast. Revocation works against every copy,

37
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:43,529
including the ones you can't reach. GitHub's
documentation says that revoking or

38
00:02:43,569 --> 00:02:48,140
rotating may be sufficient and that a
history rewrite may not be warranted.

39
00:02:48,180 --> 00:02:53,058
And the sentence to remember under pressure:
every git command typed before this

40
00:02:53,098 --> 00:02:55,640
step is time in which the key still works.

41
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:00,728
2. Assess. Five facts for the record.
Which secret, and what it can reach.

42
00:03:00,768 --> 00:03:06,163
The first commit, and the time of the first
push. Which refs contain it, a ref being

43
00:03:06,203 --> 00:03:09,915
a name that points at a commit,
such as a branch or a tag.

44
00:03:09,955 --> 00:03:14,638
Who could read it. And whether it was
used. Git answers the middle three.

45
00:03:14,678 --> 00:03:18,390
Whether it was used is answered
only by the issuer's logs.

46
00:03:18,730 --> 00:03:23,914
Now the three claims of the report. Before
the evidence, give your own verdict on

47
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:26,300
each. True or false? Say it out loud.

48
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:35,711
The three claims, against the evidence.
"The branch is clean now": a deletion

49
00:03:35,751 --> 00:03:40,741
removes nothing from history. The tip, the
newest commit of the branch, has no file,

50
00:03:40,781 --> 00:03:45,711
and the history under the tip has. "It was
only my branch": --contains decides, and

51
00:03:45,751 --> 00:03:50,741
here two branches on the server reach the
commit, because a colleague based her work

52
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:55,592
on his. "The repository is private": that
limits the audience and doesn't end the

53
00:03:55,632 --> 00:03:59,005
exposure. Each claim was a
hypothesis, and each is false.

54
00:03:59,045 --> 00:04:00,742
That's the opening, answered.

55
00:04:01,082 --> 00:04:06,223
3. Eradicate. Remove the secret from
current code, and decide whether to rewrite

56
00:04:06,263 --> 00:04:11,468
history. Rewrite when the data stays harmful
after rotation, or when the affected

57
00:04:11,508 --> 00:04:16,907
history is small and unmerged, as here.
Don't rewrite by reflex for a revoked key on

58
00:04:16,947 --> 00:04:22,282
a busy main: a rewrite costs every collaborator
their clone, changes every recorded

59
00:04:22,322 --> 00:04:24,419
commit ID and removes signatures.

60
00:04:24,759 --> 00:04:30,044
The documented tool for a whole repository
is git-filter-repo 2.47 or later with

61
00:04:30,084 --> 00:04:35,435
--sensitive-data-removal. It isn't installed
here, and one short, unmerged branch

62
00:04:35,475 --> 00:04:41,026
needs only an interactive rebase that edits
the adding commit and drops the deleting

63
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:46,351
one. A rebase copies commits, and the
interactive form lets you edit the list of

64
00:04:46,391 --> 00:04:47,217
copies first.

65
00:04:47,557 --> 00:04:52,783
After the forced push, the rewritten branch
is a new row of commits with new IDs,

66
00:04:52,823 --> 00:04:56,424
and the colleague's branch
still stands on the old ones.

67
00:04:56,464 --> 00:05:01,041
A merge or a plain pull from a stale
clone brings the old commits back.

68
00:05:01,081 --> 00:05:02,276
That was video 168.

69
00:05:02,616 --> 00:05:07,960
So every branch built on the old commits
is transplanted with git rebase --onto

70
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:09,391
<new> <old> <branch>.

71
00:05:09,731 --> 00:05:14,977
4. Recover. Deploy the new credential.
Then remove the old objects, which a force

72
00:05:15,017 --> 00:05:20,394
push doesn't do: in the picture they're
dashed, because no branch reaches them, and

73
00:05:20,434 --> 00:05:21,503
they still exist.

74
00:05:21,843 --> 00:05:26,422
In the sandbox you administer the server
and run git gc --prune=now there.

75
00:05:26,462 --> 00:05:31,416
On GitHub that's a request to Support,
who need the first changed commit and the

76
00:05:31,456 --> 00:05:36,598
number of affected pull requests and who
assist only where rotation cannot mitigate

77
00:05:36,638 --> 00:05:41,467
the risk. GitHub states that old commits
can otherwise stay reachable by ID in

78
00:05:41,507 --> 00:05:46,336
cached views, in forks and through pull
requests. Each clone keeps the objects

79
00:05:46,376 --> 00:05:51,579
through its reflogs until it is re-cloned
or its reflogs are expired and its objects

80
00:05:51,619 --> 00:05:52,016
pruned.

81
00:05:52,356 --> 00:05:58,121
5. Communicate. The team is told which
branches were rewritten and exactly what to

82
00:05:58,161 --> 00:06:03,784
do with a clone that fetched them. The
CTO is told the exposure window, what the

83
00:06:03,824 --> 00:06:09,660
credential could reach, what the issuer's
logs show, and that rotation is complete.

84
00:06:09,700 --> 00:06:13,483
Concealment is the one choice
that makes a leak worse.

85
00:06:13,823 --> 00:06:17,356
6. Prevent. An ignore rule
in the project template.

86
00:06:17,396 --> 00:06:22,682
Staging by name and reading git diff
--cached. Push protection, which blocks

87
00:06:22,722 --> 00:06:26,396
recognised token formats and
not free-form passwords.

88
00:06:26,436 --> 00:06:28,358
And short-lived credentials.

89
00:06:28,698 --> 00:06:33,597
What must never be claimed before it is
verified. That the secret is gone, and that

90
00:06:33,637 --> 00:06:38,417
it is safe. "Gone" is a statement about
every repository: no ref reaches a commit

91
00:06:38,457 --> 00:06:43,177
with the file, and the object no longer
exists, on the server and in each clone.

92
00:06:43,217 --> 00:06:47,283
"Safe" is a statement about the
issuer: the old password is rejected.

93
00:06:47,323 --> 00:06:51,270
Verification of the step that matters
is in the provider's console.

94
00:06:51,610 --> 00:06:57,155
The root cause. An unignored file with a
secret in the working tree, staged by git

95
00:06:57,195 --> 00:07:01,718
add .. Layers, three of them. Git
stores it. The issuer accepts it.

96
00:07:01,758 --> 00:07:03,012
GitHub may keep it.

97
00:07:03,352 --> 00:07:09,062
Severity. SEV 1 until rotation is confirmed.
Raise the level the moment a secret is

98
00:07:09,102 --> 00:07:11,972
involved. Lowering it later costs nothing.

99
00:07:13,812 --> 00:07:18,568
A picture helps. Think of a house key that
was photographed and posted on a notice

100
00:07:18,608 --> 00:07:21,902
board. Taking the photograph
down is the history rewrite.

101
00:07:21,942 --> 00:07:26,405
It's worth doing, and it does nothing
about the people who already copied the

102
00:07:26,445 --> 00:07:31,318
photograph. Changing the lock is the rotation.
After the lock is changed, every copy

103
00:07:31,358 --> 00:07:34,769
of the photograph is a picture
of a key that opens nothing.

104
00:07:35,109 --> 00:07:38,827
So the order follows from one
question, and it's your quiz.

105
00:07:38,867 --> 00:07:44,049
Which step works against copies you can't
reach: A, taking the photograph down, or

106
00:07:44,089 --> 00:07:46,214
B, changing the lock? Your answer?

107
00:07:51,054 --> 00:07:52,171
B. Only the lock.

108
00:07:52,511 --> 00:07:55,774
Where the picture breaks: a
notice board is one place.

109
00:07:55,814 --> 00:08:00,546
A Git repository is many. The photograph
is on the server, in every clone that

110
00:08:00,586 --> 00:08:05,439
fetched, in every branch built on top,
and on GitHub in views and refs that only

111
00:08:05,479 --> 00:08:10,150
Support can clear. That's why "taken
down" has to be verified per repository.

112
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:15,017
And the model for the message to the team:
write it for the colleague who will read

113
00:08:15,057 --> 00:08:19,475
only the commands. If the message says
"please be careful with your clones", they

114
00:08:19,515 --> 00:08:20,465
will run git pull.

115
00:08:22,305 --> 00:08:26,780
Look at the first column: no Git command.
Look at the last line of the second

116
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:31,470
column: also at the provider. The two
questions a CTO cares about most, "does it

117
00:08:31,510 --> 00:08:35,047
still work" and "was it used",
are both answered outside Git.

118
00:08:36,887 --> 00:08:40,131
Into the lab. From here on the
screen shows the solution.

119
00:08:40,171 --> 00:08:44,740
Before the first command, say it aloud as
you would in the incident channel: the

120
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:48,831
password has been rotated at the
provider, and the old one is rejected.

121
00:08:53,671 --> 00:08:58,646
In the sandbox there's no provider. In a
real incident nothing below starts until

122
00:08:58,686 --> 00:09:00,008
that sentence is true.

123
00:09:00,348 --> 00:09:01,850
Find. 🟢 SAFE.

124
00:09:02,190 --> 00:09:07,963
Two commits on any branch touched the file:
76aa6c4 added it, c5d0303 removed it.

125
00:09:08,003 --> 00:09:12,483
The fetch also shows two new
branches. Remember the second one.

126
00:09:12,823 --> 00:09:18,963
Try it now, in any repository of your own.
Thirty seconds, and it's read-only: git

127
00:09:19,003 --> 00:09:24,012
log --all --oneline -- .env. Predict
first: does it print anything?

128
00:09:28,852 --> 00:09:33,033
No output means that no commit on any
ref of that clone touches the path.

129
00:09:33,073 --> 00:09:37,716
And if it printed commits, you know the
order: the first step is at the provider.

130
00:09:38,056 --> 00:09:42,770
Scope. Predict: how many branches on
the server reach the adding commit?

131
00:09:42,810 --> 00:09:43,827
Say it out loud.

132
00:09:48,667 --> 00:09:53,537
Each line answers one claim. Two branches
reach the commit: "only my branch" is

133
00:09:53,577 --> 00:09:57,391
false, because Asha's
feature/digest-template is built on his.

134
00:09:57,431 --> 00:10:02,426
Two snapshots contain the file. The pushing
clone's reflog, its local list of the

135
00:10:02,466 --> 00:10:07,460
values a ref has had, says with dates since
when the server has had it: the first

136
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:12,371
push at 10:19. And the last two commands
are the answer to "the branch is clean

137
00:10:12,411 --> 00:10:17,157
now": the tip has no .env, and the history
under the tip prints the password.

138
00:10:17,497 --> 00:10:22,224
Rewrite, in Ravi's clone. The affected
history is small and unmerged, so an

139
00:10:22,264 --> 00:10:27,118
interactive rebase is the smaller tool.
🟡 CAUTION: git rebase -i replaces the

140
00:10:27,158 --> 00:10:32,267
branch's commits with new ones. The old
tip stays in the reflog and on the server

141
00:10:32,307 --> 00:10:33,220
until the push.

142
00:10:33,560 --> 00:10:38,547
One practical detail first: the local .env
is untracked now, and the rebase will

143
00:10:38,587 --> 00:10:41,877
check out a commit that
contains a file of that name.

144
00:10:41,917 --> 00:10:46,966
Git refuses to overwrite an untracked file,
so the local copy is moved out of the

145
00:10:47,006 --> 00:10:47,657
repository.

146
00:10:50,997 --> 00:10:55,486
Then the todo list: edit on the commit that
added the file, drop on the commit that

147
00:10:55,526 --> 00:10:58,215
removed it. The rebase
stops at the adding commit.

148
00:10:58,555 --> 00:11:03,203
The commit is amended so that it never
contained the file and adds the ignore rule

149
00:11:03,243 --> 00:11:05,548
instead. Then the remaining picks replay.

150
00:11:05,888 --> 00:11:07,780
Check the rewrite before publishing.

151
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:11,714
Four commits instead of five.
The log for the path is empty.

152
00:11:11,754 --> 00:11:16,499
git range-diff, which pairs each old
commit with its new counterpart, shows the

153
00:11:16,539 --> 00:11:21,587
whole operation. The first pair is marked
with an exclamation mark, changed, and the

154
00:11:21,627 --> 00:11:25,403
inner diff shows the .env lines
gone and the ignore rule added.

155
00:11:25,443 --> 00:11:29,279
Two pairs are equal. And the
deleting commit has no counterpart.

156
00:11:29,319 --> 00:11:31,096
The local file is now ignored.

157
00:11:34,436 --> 00:11:39,350
Publish. 🔴 DANGEROUS: a forced push. What
it changes: the server's branch, to the

158
00:11:39,390 --> 00:11:44,366
rewritten series. What it can destroy:
commits on that branch that are not in this

159
00:11:44,406 --> 00:11:49,320
clone. Preview: git fetch, then compare.
Recovery: the old tip is in this clone's

160
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:54,457
reflog. When appropriate: here, on an unmerged
branch, after the team has been told.

161
00:11:54,797 --> 00:11:59,459
The dependent branch. Asha's branch
still stands on the old commits.

162
00:11:59,499 --> 00:12:05,129
If she merges or pulls, the secret is back.
In her clone: 🟡 CAUTION, a rebase with

163
00:12:05,169 --> 00:12:05,613
--onto.

164
00:12:05,953 --> 00:12:09,780
Predict: what is the second argument,
and where does it come from?

165
00:12:09,820 --> 00:12:10,717
Say it out loud.

166
00:12:15,557 --> 00:12:20,016
The new base is the rewritten branch. The
old base is the previous value of the

167
00:12:20,056 --> 00:12:24,571
remote-tracking branch, her clone's record
of the server's branch, read from its

168
00:12:24,611 --> 00:12:29,354
reflog: the entry before the forced update.
Only her own commit is replayed, and her

169
00:12:29,394 --> 00:12:31,062
branch is pushed with a lease.

170
00:12:31,402 --> 00:12:36,255
Quick quiz before the next command.
Both branches are rewritten and pushed.

171
00:12:36,295 --> 00:12:40,691
Is the old commit A, gone from the
server, or B, still stored there?

172
00:12:40,731 --> 00:12:41,474
Your answer?

173
00:12:46,314 --> 00:12:47,603
The server still has it.

174
00:12:47,943 --> 00:12:50,960
B. No ref on the server
reaches the old commits now.

175
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,839
The object is still there, and git
show still prints the password.

176
00:12:54,879 --> 00:12:58,131
This is the moment at which
nobody may say "it is gone".

177
00:12:58,471 --> 00:13:03,377
You are the administrator of this server.
On GitHub this step is a request to GitHub

178
00:13:03,417 --> 00:13:03,848
Support.

179
00:13:04,188 --> 00:13:10,056
The clones. 🔴 DANGEROUS: git reflog expire
--expire=now --all followed by git gc

180
00:13:10,096 --> 00:13:15,963
--prune=now destroys that clone's entire
safety net: every commit and stash that

181
00:13:16,003 --> 00:13:21,723
only a reflog was keeping. There is no
preview that makes it safe and no undo.

182
00:13:21,763 --> 00:13:27,926
It is appropriate only here: purging sensitive
data from a clone after rotation, and

183
00:13:27,966 --> 00:13:30,731
after each owner has saved their work.

184
00:13:31,071 --> 00:13:34,583
Before: git cat-file -t still
answers commit in a clone.

185
00:13:34,623 --> 00:13:39,593
Every clone that ever fetched the branch
keeps the objects through its reflogs.

186
00:13:39,633 --> 00:13:41,686
After: "Not a valid object name".

187
00:13:42,026 --> 00:13:42,677
Verify.

188
00:13:43,017 --> 00:13:47,827
No commit on any ref touches the path. The
graph is one line: Asha's commit on top

189
00:13:47,867 --> 00:13:52,796
of the rewritten branch. The check goes
through the server and all three clones, for

190
00:13:52,836 --> 00:13:56,878
refs and for the blob itself, the
object that holds the file's bytes.

191
00:14:00,218 --> 00:14:04,571
Now read the last line of the check
before PASS. Read it out loud.

192
00:14:09,411 --> 00:14:14,357
"Git state only. If the credential was not
revoked or rotated first, the incident is

193
00:14:14,397 --> 00:14:17,503
still open." The check script
can't see the provider.

194
00:14:17,543 --> 00:14:18,453
Neither can Git.

195
00:14:18,793 --> 00:14:24,049
The message to the team. It names the two
rewritten branches and gives commands, not

196
00:14:24,089 --> 00:14:28,967
advice. Don't pull or merge these branches.
Fetch. If you have work on either,

197
00:14:29,007 --> 00:14:33,318
replay only your own commits with
git rebase --onto from the old tip.

198
00:14:33,358 --> 00:14:38,614
Then expire and prune, or re-clone. And
check that asking for the type of the adding

199
00:14:38,654 --> 00:14:43,091
commit fails. Compare it with the
message you wrote before the debrief.

200
00:14:43,431 --> 00:14:48,196
The postmortem line. Blameless and specific:
the developer reported it himself.

201
00:14:48,236 --> 00:14:50,386
That behavior is the one to protect.

202
00:14:52,226 --> 00:14:53,874
Five mistakes to watch for.

203
00:14:54,214 --> 00:14:59,181
Starting with Git. Root cause: the damage
happens where the secret is accepted, so

204
00:14:59,221 --> 00:15:03,761
every command typed before revocation
is time in which the key still works.

205
00:15:04,101 --> 00:15:09,275
Accepting "only my branch". Root cause:
reachability decides, and a colleague's

206
00:15:09,315 --> 00:15:14,357
branch built on the commit reaches it
too; git branch -r --contains shows it.

207
00:15:14,697 --> 00:15:19,774
Saying "it is gone" after the forced push.
Root cause: a forced push moves refs; the

208
00:15:19,814 --> 00:15:24,038
objects remain on the server and in
every clone until they are pruned.

209
00:15:24,378 --> 00:15:27,594
Rewriting the branch and
forgetting the branch built on it.

210
00:15:27,634 --> 00:15:32,120
Root cause: the dependent branch still
descends from the old commits, and its next

211
00:15:32,160 --> 00:15:33,610
